(Chapter One) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Habit of Reading) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Reading in a second language is rarely a matter of understanding every single) Tj T*
(word. Most successful readers tolerate a certain amount of ambiguity, allowing an) Tj T*
(unfamiliar expression to remain obscure until the surrounding sentence quietly) Tj T*
(explains it. This tolerance is not laziness; it is a strategy, and like every) Tj T*
(strategy it improves with deliberate practice.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although the digital age has made access to information almost ubiquitous, the) Tj T*
(capacity to sit with a difficult paragraph, to resist the temptation to skim, and) Tj T*
(to return to a sentence until its architecture becomes visible remains the rarest) Tj T*
(and most decisive skill in educated life. The habit is not glamorous. It is) Tj T*
(meticulous, repetitive, and occasionally tedious, yet its cumulative effect is) Tj T*
(substantial.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A reader who pauses to ask why a writer chose one verb rather than another is) Tj T*
(already doing something different from a reader who merely consumes pages. The) Tj T*
(first reader perceives structure; the second perceives only content. Over a year,) Tj T*
(the difference between them is not a matter of talent but of attention, and) Tj T*
(attention can be trained.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
