(Chapter Two) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Architecture of Attention) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Every long sentence is a small building. It has a foundation, which is the main) Tj T*
(clause, and it has extensions, which are the subordinate clauses, the participial) Tj T*
(phrases, and the parenthetical remarks that cluster around the foundation like) Tj T*
(scaffolding. When the building collapses under the weight of its own decoration,) Tj T*
(the reader must reconstruct it deliberately, beam by beam.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Consider a sentence that begins with a concession and ends with a qualification.) Tj T*
(The concession prepares the ground, the main clause delivers the claim, and the) Tj T*
(qualification limits it. If you can identify those three movements, you can) Tj T*
(usually paraphrase the sentence even when several words remain unknown. This is) Tj T*
(why structural awareness is more valuable than vocabulary size alone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The most resilient readers are those who have learned to tolerate uncertainty) Tj T*
(without losing the thread. They do not stop at the first unfamiliar word, and they) Tj T*
(do not pretend that the word is unimportant. Instead they mark it, continue) Tj T*
(reading, and return later with a hypothesis that the rest of the paragraph can) Tj T*
(confirm or reject. Such a reader is skeptical but not paralyzed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
