(Chapter Three) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Learning to Notice) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Noticing is the moment when an input becomes an object of attention. A reader may) Tj T*
(see the word "inevitable" a hundred times and never notice it, and then one day,) Tj T*
(because a teacher circled it or because a character used it in an argument, the) Tj T*
(word becomes visible. From that moment it is available for learning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Teachers can accelerate this process by preparing students before they read. A) Tj T*
(short pre-study of the hardest sentences and the most opaque vocabulary gives the) Tj T*
(reader a map, and a map changes the experience of a difficult terrain. The reader) Tj T*
(still has to walk, but no longer has to guess where the path might be.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The aim is not to eliminate difficulty, since difficulty is where learning) Tj T*
(happens. The aim is to make difficulty navigable. A chapter that once felt) Tj T*
(impenetrable becomes a sequence of problems that can be solved one at a time, and) Tj T*
(the confidence that follows is not an illusion but evidence, accumulated sentence) Tj T*
(by sentence, that the reader is becoming capable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
